SLITHER SMART CONTRACT SECURITY ANALYSIS
Phase 1 - Step 3: Install and Run Slither


1. CHECK PYTHON

python --version

Purpose:
Checks whether Python is installed and displays the installed Python version.


2. INSTALL SLITHER

python -m pip install slither-analyzer

Purpose:
Installs Slither, a security analysis tool for Solidity smart contracts.


3. CHECK SLITHER

slither --version

Purpose:
Checks whether Windows can find the Slither command.

Note:
If this says that "slither" is not recognized, it usually means the Python Scripts folder is not in PATH.


4. CHECK SLITHER THROUGH PYTHON

python -m slither --version

Purpose:
Runs Slither through Python without depending on the Windows PATH configuration.


5. VIEW THE PYTHON SCRIPTS FOLDER

Get-ChildItem "C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Python\pythoncore-3.14-64\Scripts"

Purpose:
Lists the programs installed by Python.

Important files may include:

slither.exe
solc-select.exe
solc.exe


6. RUN SLITHER DIRECTLY FROM THE SCRIPTS FOLDER

cd "C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Python\pythoncore-3.14-64\Scripts"

.\slither.exe --version

Purpose:
Runs Slither directly from the folder where slither.exe is installed.

The .\ means "run the program from the current folder."


7. CHECK SOLC-SELECT

.\solc-select.exe --version

Purpose:
Checks the installed solc-select version.

solc-select is used to install and switch between Solidity compiler versions.


8. INSTALL SOLIDITY COMPILER 0.8.20

.\solc-select.exe install 0.8.20

Purpose:
Downloads and installs Solidity compiler version 0.8.20.


9. SELECT SOLIDITY COMPILER 0.8.20

.\solc-select.exe use 0.8.20

Purpose:
Tells the system to use Solidity compiler version 0.8.20.


10. CHECK THE SOLIDITY COMPILER

.\solc.exe --version

Purpose:
Confirms that the Solidity compiler is installed and working.


11. GO TO THE PROJECT FOLDER

cd "C:\Users\YourUsername\Desktop\CMSC451"

Purpose:
Moves PowerShell into the CMSC 451 project folder.


12. CREATE THE SOLIDITY FILE

New-Item VulnerableBank.sol -ItemType File

Purpose:
Creates an empty Solidity file called:

VulnerableBank.sol


13. OPEN THE FILE IN VS CODE

code VulnerableBank.sol

Purpose:
Opens VulnerableBank.sol in Visual Studio Code.

The Solidity code can then be entered and saved in VS Code.


14. CHECK THAT THE FILE EXISTS

Get-ChildItem

Purpose:
Lists the files in the current folder.

You should see:

VulnerableBank.sol


15. RUN SLITHER ON THE CONTRACT

python -m slither VulnerableBank.sol

Purpose:
Analyzes the Solidity contract for potential security problems.

This is the main command used for Phase 1 Step 3.


16. RUN ONLY THE REENTRANCY DETECTOR

python -m slither VulnerableBank.sol --detect reentrancy-eth

Purpose:
Tells Slither to specifically check for Ether reentrancy vulnerabilities.


17. OPTIONAL: RUN SLITHER USING THE FULL PATH

& "C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Python\pythoncore-3.14-64\Scripts\slither.exe" VulnerableBank.sol

Purpose:
Runs Slither using its complete file path.

This is useful if "slither" is not recognized by PowerShell.


18. OPTIONAL: RUN THE REENTRANCY CHECK USING THE FULL PATH

& "C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Python\pythoncore-3.14-64\Scripts\slither.exe" VulnerableBank.sol --detect reentrancy-eth

Purpose:
Runs Slither from its full path and specifically checks for reentrancy.


IMPORTANT COMMANDS TO REMEMBER

cd "C:\Users\YourUsername\Desktop\CMSC451"

Moves into the project folder.


code VulnerableBank.sol

Opens the Solidity contract in VS Code.


python -m slither VulnerableBank.sol

Runs a complete Slither security analysis.


python -m slither VulnerableBank.sol --detect reentrancy-eth

Runs a specific reentrancy security check.


BASIC WORKFLOW

1. Create Solidity contract
2. Open it in VS Code
3. Write/save the contract
4. Run Slither
5. Read the security warnings
6. Determine why Slither reported each issue
7. Save the results for the project


